# Setting Up Internet Access for My AD Lab

**Date: 25 November 2026**

## The plan

I wanted to give my Active Directory lab access to the internet so I could sync it to Microsoft Entra ID and update help in powershell

I also wanted to keep my lab separate from my normal home network.

## What I did

- Created another virtual machine in TrueNAS for OPNsense.
- Installed OPNsense to use as the firewall for my lab.
- Connected it to my home network for internet access.
- Connected the other side to my isolated lab network.
- Set up the LAN and WAN interfaces.
- Configured the firewall to stop my lab accessing my home network.
- Updated the default gateway on DC01 so it could use OPNsense to reach the internet.

## Testing

 I then tested the connection from DC01.

- Used `ping 1.1.1.1` to check internet connectivity.
- Used `nslookup google.com` to check DNS was working.
- checked i could browse in Microsoft Edge
- Tried accessing my TrueNAS management address and confirmed the firewall blocked the connection. Initially the firewall did not work until i realised i needed to move my block rule above the allow one

All the tests worked after this change

## What I learned

This was my first time setting up a virtual firewall and configuring network connections between different networks.

I learned more about:
- How firewalls control network traffic.
- The difference between LAN and WAN.
- How a default gateway works.
- How firewall rules are created and why their order matters.
- How to test network connections using basic commands.

## Next steps

Now that the lab has internet access, I will sync my Active Directory with Microsoft Entra ID
